WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:24.840
[music]

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.040
Emily: "Hey, guys!

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:30.080
This is Emily Conover, physics reporter for Science News,

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:33.080
coming at you from a tent.

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:35.560
We're here in Corvallis

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.840
on a converted athletic field of some sort.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:41.560
And marveling at how blue the sky is,

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:43.720
so we hope it stays that way for the next few hours,

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:47.220
so that we can get a great view of the eclipse."

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:50.140
Emily: "Ok, check it out guys, I'm gonna look."

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.460
"Ahh! It's so cool."

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:57.600
[screams of joy and wonder]

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.460
Emily: "Oh my god, it's amazing!"

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:03.280
Emily: "The sun is back!"

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:05.680
[cheering]

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:16.280
[music]

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:18.920
Laura: "That was wild!"

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:39.480
[music]

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:41.440
Lisa: "Hi! I'm Lisa Grossman!

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:43.360
I'm the astronomy writer at Science News.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:45.120
I'm with Alyssa Boll.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:45.840
Introduce yourself."

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:46.900
Alyssa: "Hello, I'm Alyssa Boll,

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:47.800
and I'm an intern with the

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:49.760
High Altitude Observatory this summer.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:51.160
And we have three experiments

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:52.520
going on for the eclipse."

00:01:53.360 --> 00:02:09.080
[music]

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.040
Erika: "Hi, this is Erika Engelhaupt,

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.600
writer and former editor at Science News magazine.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.080
I'm enjoying the eclipse

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:16.680
from beautiful Madisonville, Tennessee,

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:19.240
in the foothills of the Smoky Mountains."

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:21.120
Gordon: "So we have one minute to see

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.560
the shadow race toward those mountains,

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:25.060
go over the mountains,

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:27.060
and go through the clouds.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:29.100
[cheers]

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:31.720
Erika: "It's gone! Woo!"

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:39.440
[music]

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:43.080
Erika: "Oh my god! We're standing in the dark."

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:52.760
[music]

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:56.600
Helen: "Ok, so it's the morning of the day of the eclipse.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.520
And the weather's looking good.

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:01.520
We've got about two or three hours to go.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.160
So, fingers crossed."

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:18.000
[music]

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:21.160
[hooting and hollering]

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:24.640
[crickets chirping]

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:48.080
[music]

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:52.320
"Happy eclipse from Columbia, South Carolina!"

00:03:52.760 --> 00:04:03.200
[music]

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:14.880
[cheering]

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:47.840
[music]

